    "body": "ResearchWithMe is now a single-author blog. Every essay is an MDX file in this repository, rendered at\nbuild time and published by pushing to main. There is no account system, no database, and no admin\npanel — the writing is the site, and git is the only publishing interface.\n\n<KeyTakeaways>\n\n- One author, one archive: posts are written by me and published from git.\n- Content is MDX, so prose can use figures, callouts, video, tables, and highlighted code.\n- Everything is statically generated. There is no login, no database, and no runtime write path.\n\n</KeyTakeaways>\n\nWhat an essay on this site is made of, pinned to the places a reader meets it:\n\n<WelcomeAnatomyAtlas />\n\n\n## Why change what already worked?\n\nThe previous version was built to host many writers: applications, roles, an editorial dashboard, a\ndraft studio, and a moderation queue. For a blog written by one person, all of that was machinery\naround the actual work. Removing it made the site smaller, faster, and considerably easier to reason\nabout.\n\nWhat is left is the part that matters: a reading experience, a durable archive, and a publishing\nworkflow that is just git. The pages are pre-rendered with the Next.js App Router, so the essay text\nis in the first response rather than assembled by a script in the browser\n([Next.js docs](https://nextjs.org/docs/app)).\n\n## How does publishing work now?\n\nA post is one file:\n\n```mdx\n---\ntitle: \"A post about something\"\ndescription: \"One or two sentences for cards, search, and the feed.\"\ndate: \"2026-11-24\"\ntags: [\"reproducible-research\", \"methodology\"]\n---\n\nThe body is MDX. Components are available without imports.\n```\n\nMDX is Markdown that compiles to JSX, which is why the prose can use a figure or a callout without a\nbespoke page template ([MDX docs](https://mdxjs.com/docs/)). Add the file, commit it, push. The build\nvalidates the frontmatter with Zod, renders the page, and updates the index\n([Zod docs](https://zod.dev)). If a field is wrong, the build fails with the file name and the field —\nno silently missing post.\n\nPublishing is the easy part; staying honest is enforced. The gates every page ships under, as a sortable table:\n\n<WelcomeGatesMatrix />\n\nThe page-weight budget itself, as a partition you can drag — the caps are the real §9 limits; only the split is illustrative:\n\n<WelcomeBudgetSplit />\n\n\n<Callout tone=\"note\" title=\"Drafts stay local\">\n  A post with <code>draft: true</code> appears when the site runs in development and is excluded from a\n  production build. You can write in public view without publishing.\n</Callout>\n\n## What carried over from the old site?\n\nThe things that were worth keeping stayed: the typography, the paper-and-ink palette, the reading\nprogress bar, the theme control, and the archive-first navigation. Bookmarks, accounts, the studio,\nand the research-network surfaces did not — they existed to support a multi-writer platform that is no\nlonger here.\n\nCheck yourself on how the machine works — every answer is on this page:\n\n<WelcomeQuiz />\n\nIf you are looking for something that used to be at a specific address, the [archive](/blog) and\n[search](/search) are the places to start. The [RSS feed](/rss.xml) still works, and it is still the\nbest way to follow new writing. If you want the longer version of why this is a one-person site, the\n[about page](/about) explains it."
